  "S5": {
   "id": "S5",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Ml Lecture Notes",
   "text": "5. Data Splitting and Cross-Validation Data is usually divided into a training set for fitting, a validation set for tuning hyperparameters and a test set used once for the final unbiased estimate. Using the test set repeatedly for decisions leaks information and makes the estimate optimistic. Data leakage more generally means that information from outside the training data, such as future values or the target itself, influences the features; it produces unrealistically good scores that collapse in production. k-fold cross-validation splits the training data into k equal folds. The model is trained k times, each time holding out a different fold for validation, and the k scores are averaged. Typical values are k = 5 or k = 10. Stratified k-fold preserves the class proportions in every fold and is recommended for classification with imbalanced classes.",
   "doc_id": "ml_lecture_notes",
